{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## OPERATOR RULING — this does NOT gate epic e369\n\nAsked directly whether e369's closure should be gated on fixing this, the operator ruled **follow-up, do\nnot gate**. Recorded here because the operator's earlier ruling on the three J11-surfaced defects (751e,\n2b16, 88d9) went the STRICTEST way, so a reader could reasonably expect the same here; this one was\ndecided differently, deliberately.\n\nThe distinction that justifies it: the behaviour introduced by 2b16 fails **SAFE**. G5 declines to unlink\nwhen the pair's net-active relation is not the one whose peer link vanished, so it never removes a link\nthe peer still carries — the alternative would be destroying local data. What remains is a\n**non-convergence**: the removal re-emits and re-declines every pass, visible only as a log line. That is\na materially different class from the J11 defects, every one of which silently produced a WRONG result.\n\nAnd the proper fix is a design pass in its own right, not a patch: expressing \"remove only the `blocks`\nedge between A and B\" means changing the public `rebar.unlink(id1, id2)` signature or introducing a new\nlower-level seam. Pulling that into e369 would delay DC delivery for a narrow case that already fails in\nthe safe direction."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785729839745583001,"uuid":"5f6b26b1-3d14-4463-a329-e24b84dacfb7"}